  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>The Carius halogen method in analytical chemistry is a method for the quantitative determination of halogens in chemical substances. A known mass of an organic compound is heated with fuming nitric acid in the presence of silver nitrate contained in a hard glass tube known as Carius tube, in a furnace.</p><p>Mass of organic compound&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>45</mn><mi>gm</mi></math></p><p>Mass of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>AgBr</mi></math> obtained&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>36</mn><mi>gm</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;</mo></math>Moles of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>AgBr</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>36</mn></mrow><mn>188</mn></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;</mo></math> Mass of Bromine&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>36</mn></mrow><mn>188</mn></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>80</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1532</mn><mi>gm</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>%</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Br</mi></math>&nbsp;in compound&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1532</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>45</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>34</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>04</mn><mo>%</mo></math></p></blockquote>",
